# {{GAME_NAME}}

A standalone static browser game. The starter includes Daily/Practice, local bests, pause, replay, bilingual results, Threads/X/WhatsApp drafts, Instagram Story images and manual save/copy fallbacks. There is no backend or runtime package dependency.

## Make it your game

1. Edit `public/game-config.json`: game name, description, rules, action label, share wording, palette and round length. Keep `id` and `challengeId` unique for this title.
2. Replace `public/assets/game.js`: export `create(seed, config)`, `step(state, input, seconds)`, `draw(context, state, options)` and `result(state, language)`.
3. Put your own images under `public/assets/`; keep drawing assets on the same origin so result images can be exported.

The included ten-second order-stamping game is a working example. Changing the config changes branding/copy; changing `game.js` changes the mechanism.

`create` returns serializable state with `elapsed`, `duration`, `score` and `status: 'running'`. `step` receives `{held, presses}`; it may mutate state or return a replacement. Set `status: 'finished'` when done. `draw` paints an 820 × 500 canvas; options include `reduced`, `idle` and `colors`. Keep drawing free of state mutations. `result` returns `{score, title, stats: [{label, value}]}`. The shell owns timers, controls, local bests, reproducible links, result UI and sharing.

## Run locally

```sh
npm ci
npm run dev
npm run build
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:4173`. If another local game is using that port, run `PORT=4174 npm run dev`.

The build refreshes marked game names/descriptions from the config. You may add `socialImage` with a local path such as `/assets/social-cover.png` to the config; create that image yourself for link previews. The build checks that configured cover files exist. Score images and 9:16 Story images are generated in the browser regardless of the link-preview cover.

## Sharing and limits

- Threads, X and WhatsApp open drafts. The player completes posting in that service; the website never posts automatically.
- Instagram Story uses a 1080 × 1920 PNG. Players can use their device share sheet or save/upload it and add the copied URL with a Link sticker. Browser and installed-app support varies.
- Local/private URLs are marked as preview links. External draft buttons activate on a public HTTPS address.
- Scores are casual, client-side claims. A verified leaderboard or prizes require server validation.
- Clipboard denial exposes a selectable link. Missing native sharing keeps download/copy available. Starting another run invalidates old pending images.

## Security and ads

`public/_headers` supplies CSP and browser protection headers; local preview applies the same rules. Scripts load from this site. Blob images are allowed for score previews. Inline CSS is allowed for the canvas UI's dynamic styling; inline scripts, objects and forms are blocked.

Advertising starts disabled. The copied ad adapter requires real publisher settings and consent. The current CSP intentionally blocks external ad scripts: review the required ad/consent policy and update privacy/CSP before enabling advertisements. Keep secrets outside `public/` and use two-factor authentication on hosting/domain accounts.

To create another clean starter using the same flow:

```sh
npm run new-game -- another-game --name "Another Game"
```

The command creates a new folder under `games/` and refuses to overwrite an existing folder. It resets site and advertising settings for the new title. Fonts remain self-hosted with their licenses.

## Games menu

Add a `games` list to `public/game-config.json` to link your games by name:

```json
"games": [
  {"name": "Noodle Rush", "href": "/"},
  {"name": "Snack Sneak", "href": "https://your-published-game.example/"}
]
```

Use local paths or public HTTPS URLs. Without the list, the menu shows this game. `npm run build` refreshes page metadata and the app manifest from the config. Keep player-facing copy short.

## Release checks

Run `npm run check` to validate the game locally. Test gameplay and native sharing on your phone too.

## Optional PostHog analytics

The generated shell includes Play-activated visits, rounds, active timing, scores, replays and share-click events. Set `analytics.enabled`, public `analytics.projectToken` (`phc_…`) and `analytics.region` (`us` or `eu`) in `public/site-config.json` after creating a PostHog project. No SDK, autocapture or recording is loaded. Default is disabled; local previews and GPC/Do Not Track never send events. Privacy text and an EN/HK/TW footer toggle are included. Existing opt-outs remain off. New projects reset this configuration. Check the real public site and PostHog live events after deployment; the integration cannot create your account.
